00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:01.189 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  across europe the pace of vaccination is  increasing  more jabs are going in more doses are  being delivered  there are now four vaccines authorized  for use in the european union  but one of them astrazeneca has had  clearly the roughest ride  rows over possible rare side effects  have led to its use being restricted by  some countries  but the greater anger is about the  company delivering  far fewer doses than the eu ordered  today quietly but combatively  the eu confirmed it was taking legal  action  the terms of the country or some terms  of the contract have not been respected  and the company has not been in a  position  to come up with a reliable strategy to  ensure  the timely delivery of doses what  matters to us  in this case is that we want to make  sure that there is a speedy delivery  of a sufficient number of doses that  european citizens are entitled to  and which have been promised on the  basis of the contract  the company responded quickly and  vehemently  saying astrazeneca has fully complied  with the advanced purchase agreement  with the european commission  and will strongly defend itself in court  we believe any litigation is without  merit and we welcome this opportunity  to resolve this dispute as soon as  possible  but at the heart of this is one rather  woolly expression here's a section  from the european contract with  astrazeneca the first paragraph talks  about the company making  best reasonable efforts to make vaccines  within the eu  the fourth paragraph says that  commitment should include using  factories  within the uk again it says best  reasonable efforts a phrase that is at  once  vague but apparently crucial  litigation takes a long time you've got  to have  a process of everybody producing witness  statements there'll be an enormous  amount of evidence to look at  you're talking about all the emails or  the phone attendance notes  all the whatsapps if those exist all the  documents between the parties  there may be hundreds of statements to  take  it's complicated and it is very time  consuming and it is very expensive  europe was supposed to get 300 million  doses of astrazeneca by the end of june  in fact it will receive a third of that  and none  have been sent from uk factories in  brussels there is  genuine anger and resentment  legal action really offers a quick  solution to commercial rails  and it won't happen here this rancorous  dispute  could yet drag on for years adam parsons  sky news  brussels

